1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,450
This lesson develops an interview-ready approach to Fault Tolerance: Core model and tradeoffs. The central model is: Keep

2
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:24,950
critical behavior within acceptable limits when dependencies, nodes, or regions fail. Begin by clarifying the product behavior rather

3
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:37,450
than naming technologies. Ask who uses the system, what the critical write and read paths are, which operations

4
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:49,950
must be correct, and which outcomes may be delayed. State two or three non-goals. This prevents a familiar

5
00:00:50,000 --> 00:01:02,450
architecture from being forced onto the wrong product. Separate functional requirements from quality attributes. Functional requirements describe user-visible

6
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:14,950
actions and state changes. Quality attributes describe scale,

7
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:27,450
latency, availability, durability, consistency, privacy, and cost. Pick the two attributes that drive architecture and say why. Avoid

8
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:39,950
promising maximum consistency, maximum availability, minimum latency, and minimum cost simultaneously. A strong interview answer makes the tradeoff

9
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:52,450
visible and ties it to a user consequence. Use rounded assumptions for daily active users, peak requests per

10
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:04,950
second, read-to-write ratio, payload size, retention, and replication. Calculate only values that influence a decision. Label every assumption

11
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:17,450
and keep units on the page. An estimate is useful when it changes partition count, storage tier, cache

12
00:02:17,500 --> 00:02:29,950
capacity, or network design. It is not useful

13
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:42,450
when it creates false precision. Add headroom and distinguish average from peak load. Build the design around these

14
00:02:42,500 --> 00:02:54,950
decisions: Timeouts, retries, circuit breakers, bulkheads, redundancy, and graceful degradation. Draw clients, edge controls, stateless services, authoritative stores,

15
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:07,450
asynchronous paths, and observability. Put arrows in the direction of data flow and label synchronous versus asynchronous work.

16
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:19,950
For each durable write, identify the source of truth. For each cache or index, identify how it becomes

17
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:32,450
fresh enough. For each queue, identify the consumer's idempotency strategy. Define the smallest useful API and the core

18
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:44,950
data entities. Include identifiers, ownership, timestamps or versions,

19
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:57,450
and uniqueness constraints. Explain the dominant query patterns before selecting storage. If data is partitioned, name the partition

20
00:03:57,500 --> 00:04:09,950
key and show why it spreads load without breaking essential queries. If an operation crosses partitions or services,

21
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:22,450
describe the consistency boundary and how partial completion is reconciled. Pressure-test the design against Retry storms, correlated failure,

22
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:34,950
false redundancy, and silent data loss. Walk through timeout, retry, dependency outage, overload, and recovery. Bound every retry

23
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:47,450
with deadlines, backoff, and jitter. Use idempotency where a repeated request can create a duplicate effect. Define whether

24
00:04:47,500 --> 00:04:59,950
the system fails closed, fails open, serves stale

25
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:12,450
data, queues work, or rejects it. Then explain how operators detect the condition and how the system returns

26
00:05:12,500 --> 00:05:24,950
to normal. For every major component, connect the choice to a requirement and name the cost it introduces.

27
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:37,450
A cache reduces latency but creates freshness and invalidation work. Replication improves resilience and read capacity but introduces

28
00:05:37,500 --> 00:05:49,950
lag, conflict, and failover decisions. Partitioning increases scale but complicates queries, transactions, and rebalancing. Asynchronous work absorbs bursts

29
00:05:50,000 --> 00:06:02,450
but changes completion semantics and requires retries, deduplication, and backlog operations. This requirement-choice-cost pattern keeps the answer grounded

30
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:14,950
and prevents architecture by buzzword. Start with the

31
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:27,450
simplest architecture that satisfies the stated scale. Then identify the first bottleneck and evolve one dimension at a

32
00:06:27,500 --> 00:06:39,950
time: add caching for read pressure, partition state for size or write throughput, queue noncritical work for burst

33
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:52,450
absorption, and add regional redundancy only when the availability objective justifies the cost. This evolution story demonstrates judgment

34
00:06:52,500 --> 00:07:04,950
better than drawing every distributed component from the beginning. Define service-level indicators for latency, availability, correctness, freshness, and

35
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:17,450
backlog. Alert on user impact and exhaustion trends, not every internal fluctuation. Carry request identifiers through logs and

36
00:07:17,500 --> 00:07:29,950
traces. For security, authenticate the caller, authorize the

37
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:42,450
resource action, encrypt in transit and at rest, minimize sensitive data, rotate credentials, and audit privileged changes. Mention

38
00:07:42,500 --> 00:07:54,950
abuse controls when the system exposes public APIs or user-generated content. Prepare to zoom into one write and

39
00:07:55,000 --> 00:08:07,450
one read at message-level detail. Name the request, validation, authoritative mutation, secondary updates, response, and recovery behavior. Then

40
00:08:07,500 --> 00:08:19,950
answer four predictable pivots: what changes at ten times scale, what happens when the main dependency is unavailable,

41
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:32,450
how duplicate requests are handled, and how a user observes a stale or partial result. If a decision

42
00:08:32,500 --> 00:08:44,950
depends on an unknown requirement, offer two branches

43
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:57,450
and ask the interviewer which product behavior to optimize. Close by restating the requirements that drove the design,

44
00:08:57,500 --> 00:09:09,950
the source of truth, the main data path, and the most important tradeoff. Offer one alternative and say

45
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:22,450
when it would be preferable. Then invite the interviewer to choose a deep dive: data model, consistency, capacity,

46
00:09:22,500 --> 00:09:34,950
failure recovery, or a product-specific edge case. The design is complete when its behavior under pressure is as

47
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:47,450
clear as its happy path. Keep the final summary short enough that the interviewer can challenge one decision

48
00:09:47,500 --> 00:09:59,950
while time remains.
